sapphire: a surface is compressed when its creator asks for it, as iOS does

iOS compresses an IOSurface because its creator asks before IOSurfaceCreate, through the format and the planes'
compression (QuartzCore, CA::SurfaceUtil::CAIOSurfaceCreate in 20H392); the name comes afterwards by IOSurfaceSetValue,
and no first access decides anything. QuartzCore asks for its framebuffers in '&BGA' once CADeviceUseCompression holds:
on this port the ChipID table refuses it by default (0x8015) and the CAEnableIOSurfaceCompression preference of
com.apple.backboardd lets it through, as the code reads it after the table. Read on the phone with it set: the
"CA Framebuffer" surfaces come '&BGA', the "CA Asynchronous Page" ones BGRA.

The scanout pool takes '&BGA' as well as BGRA, and lays a surface out UBWC only when it is '&BGA'; everything asked for
linear stays linear (0406's name test and 0407's settling by first access no longer apply). A UBWC surface's plane 0
says so where IOSurface.framework reads it: compression type 2 (QuartzCore's AGX plane description, without universal
compression) at IOSurfaceClient + 0x124, the headers' and data regions' offsets at + 0x10c / + 0x110.

---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetSurface.cpp
